</div>

</nav>


<div class="container">

<!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->

<aside class="sidebar">

<h3>Decision Lens</h3>

<div class="side-block">
<div class="side-title">What are you trying to improve?</div>
<div class="side-text">
Career, income, business, learning, lifestyle, location, or professional network.
</div>
</div>

<div class="side-block">
<div class="side-title">Start with constraints</div>
<div class="side-text">
Budget, time, transportation, responsibilities, skills, credentials and flexibility.
</div>
</div>

<div class="side-block">
<div class="side-title">Then compare options</div>
<div class="side-text">
Look at benefits, costs, uncertainty, accessibility and long-term effects.
</div>
</div>


<h3 style="margin-top:2rem;">Useful Questions</h3>

<ul class="side-list">
<li>What would improve my options?</li>
<li>What am I currently trading away?</li>
<li>What could I test before committing?</li>
<li>What information am I missing?</li>
<li>What would make this move worthwhile?</li>
</ul>

</aside>


<!-- MAIN -->

<main class="main-content">

<header class="intelligence-header">

<div class="status-bar">
Practical Strategy Library
</div>

<h1>
Strategic Intelligence
</h1>

<p>
Explore practical ways to evaluate opportunities, develop useful connections,
consider a change of location, build skills, and make decisions under uncertainty.
</p>

</header>


<!-- ORIENTATION -->

<section class="orientation">

<div class="orientation-label">
A Better Starting Point
</div>

<h2>
Good strategy does not require having every answer.
</h2>

<p>
It means asking better questions, comparing realistic options, checking the evidence,
and choosing actions that fit your actual circumstances.
</p>

</section>


<!-- OPPORTUNITIES -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-header">

<h2>Opportunity Areas</h2>

<p>Different paths can solve different problems.</p>

</div>

<div class="card-grid">

<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Career</span>

<h3>Build a Stronger Career Position</h3>

<p>
Combine skills, practical projects, credentials, networking and evidence of your
ability rather than relying on a single qualification.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Income</span>

<h3>Increase Income Options</h3>

<p>
Look for ways to increase hourly value, add complementary income streams,
develop scarce skills, or move toward better-paying roles.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Skills</span>

<h3>Create Skill Combinations</h3>

<p>
Pair technical, communication, business, analytical or operational skills
to create a broader range of opportunities.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Networking</span>

<h3>Build Useful Professional Relationships</h3>

<p>
Focus on relationships where people can exchange knowledge, referrals,
feedback, opportunities, collaboration or practical experience.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Learning</span>

<h3>Learn Through Real Projects</h3>

<p>
Turn learning into evidence by creating projects, documenting your process,
solving real problems and showing what you can produce.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Business</span>

<h3>Test Before You Invest</h3>

<p>
Validate demand with small experiments before committing significant money,
time or resources to an untested idea.
</p>

</article>

</div>

</section>


<!-- WHY MOVE -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-header">

<h2>Reasons Someone Might Consider Moving</h2>

<p>A move should solve a specific problem or create a meaningful opportunity.</p>

</div>

<div class="card-grid">

<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Employment</span>

<h3>Access a Larger Job Market</h3>

<p>
Some locations have more openings in particular industries. Compare actual
job postings, wages and hiring requirements before deciding.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Housing</span>

<h3>Change the Cost Structure</h3>

<p>
Housing can dramatically affect financial flexibility. Compare rent, utilities,
transportation and wages together rather than looking at rent alone.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Transportation</span>

<h3>Reduce Transportation Dependence</h3>

<p>
A location with reliable public transportation, walkable services or shorter
commutes may reduce the total cost of getting to work and essential services.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Industry</span>

<h3>Move Closer to Relevant Industries</h3>

<p>
Certain cities and regions have stronger concentrations of specific industries,
employers, training programs and professional events.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Education</span>

<h3>Improve Access to Learning</h3>

<p>
A move may provide better access to community colleges, workforce programs,
libraries, apprenticeships, universities or professional networks.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Lifestyle</span>

<h3>Find a Better Daily Fit</h3>

<p>
People sometimes move because they want different weather, pace of life,
community activities, recreation or access to everyday services.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Business</span>

<h3>Get Closer to Customers</h3>

<p>
For some businesses, proximity to customers, suppliers, partners or industry
clusters can create practical advantages.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Family</span>

<h3>Move Closer to Support Systems</h3>

<p>
Location decisions can also involve proximity to family, trusted relationships,
caregiving resources or communities that provide practical support.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Opportunity</span>

<h3>Expand the Opportunity Set</h3>

<p>
Sometimes the main reason to move is simply to gain access to a wider range
of jobs, training, businesses, communities or activities.
</p>

</article>

</div>

</section>


<!-- LOCATION RESEARCH -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-header">

<h2>Location Research Categories</h2>

<p>Research the environment before making a major move.</p>

</div>

<div class="location-grid">

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">01</div>
<h3>Wages</h3>
<p>Compare typical pay for the jobs you could realistically obtain.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">02</div>
<h3>Housing</h3>
<p>Compare rent, deposits, utilities and other recurring costs.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">03</div>
<h3>Transportation</h3>
<p>Evaluate transit, commute times, walking access and vehicle costs.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">04</div>
<h3>Job Availability</h3>
<p>Look at current openings rather than relying on general reputation.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">05</div>
<h3>Industry Mix</h3>
<p>Identify industries and employers with meaningful local presence.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">06</div>
<h3>Taxes</h3>
<p>Understand how state and local taxes could affect take-home income.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">07</div>
<h3>Healthcare Access</h3>
<p>Consider availability, distance, insurance networks and costs.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">08</div>
<h3>Education</h3>
<p>Research colleges, workforce programs, apprenticeships and training.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">09</div>
<h3>Community</h3>
<p>Explore organizations, events, libraries, clubs and professional groups.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">10</div>
<h3>Climate</h3>
<p>Consider weather patterns and how they affect your daily life.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">11</div>
<h3>Commute</h3>
<p>Measure actual travel time to realistic employment opportunities.</p>
</div>

<div class="location">
<div class="location-number">12</div>
<h3>Long-Term Fit</h3>
<p>Ask whether the location supports your goals beyond the first year.</p>
</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- STRATEGIC TRAVEL -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-header">

<h2>Travel With a Purpose</h2>

<p>Travel can be used for exploration, learning and relationship-building.</p>

</div>

<div class="card-grid">

<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Exploration</span>

<h3>Visit Before You Move</h3>

<p>
If possible, experience a potential location during ordinary days rather
than evaluating it only as a tourist.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Research</span>

<h3>Conduct a Local Opportunity Scan</h3>

<p>
Review employers, wages, housing, transportation, training programs and
community resources before making assumptions.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Networking</span>

<h3>Attend Industry Events</h3>

<p>
Professional associations, conferences, workshops and community events
can help you understand an industry from people working in it.
</p>

</article>


<article class="strategy-card">

<span class="tag">Learning</span>

<h3>Use Travel to Compare Environments</h3>

<p>
Pay attention to how different environments affect your productivity,
expenses, transportation and access to opportunities.
</p>

</article>

</div>

</section>


<!-- DECISION FRAMEWORK -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-header">

<h2>Before Making a Major Move</h2>

<p>Compare the decision instead of relying on a feeling.</p>

</div>

<div class="checklist">

<h3>Questions worth answering</h3>

<ul>

<li>What specific problem would the move solve?</li>

<li>What opportunity would it create?</li>

<li>What would the move cost upfront?</li>

<li>What would monthly expenses look like?</li>

<li>What jobs are actually available?</li>

<li>What wages are realistically available?</li>

<li>How would transportation work?</li>

<li>What skills or credentials are required?</li>

<li>What support systems would I have?</li>

<li>What would I give up by leaving?</li>

<li>Can I test the location before committing?</li>

<li>What happens if the move doesn't work?</li>

</ul>

</div>

</section>


<!-- DECISION TYPES -->

<section class="section">

<div class="section-header">

<h2>Four Ways to Respond to an Opportunity</h2>

<p>The right response depends on uncertainty, cost and potential value.</p>

</div>

<div class="decision-grid">

<div class="decision">
<strong>Explore</strong>
<span>Gather information before making a commitment.</span>
</div>

<div class="decision">
<strong>Test</strong>
<span>Run a small experiment to see whether the idea works.</span>
</div>

<div class="decision">
<strong>Prepare</strong>
<span>Build the skills, savings or relationships needed for later action.</span>
</div>

<div class="decision">
<strong>Act</strong>
<span>Move forward when the evidence and circumstances support the decision.</span>
</div>

<div class="decision">
<strong>Wait</strong>
<span>Delay the decision when important information is still missing.</span>
</div>

<div class="decision">
<strong>Decline</strong>
<span>Recognize when an opportunity does not fit your goals or constraints.</span>
</div>

</div>

</section>


<!-- PRINCIPLE -->

<section class="principle">

<div class="principle-label">
Strategic Principle
</div>

<h2>
A better opportunity is only useful if it fits the person, the resources and the situation.
</h2>

<p>
Good strategy is not about finding a universally “best” place, career,
network or opportunity. It is about finding an option that makes sense
for your particular goals, constraints and evidence.
</p>

</section>

</main>


<!-- RIGHT SIDEBAR -->

<aside class="sidebar">

<h3>Research Before Action</h3>

<div class="side-block">
<div class="side-title">Compare the numbers</div>
<div class="side-text">
Wages, rent, transportation, taxes and recurring expenses.
</div>
</div>

<div class="side-block">
<div class="side-title">Check the opportunity</div>
<div class="side-text">
Look at actual employers, programs, customers, industries and openings.
</div>
</div>

<div class="side-block">
<div class="side-title">Consider the transition</div>
<div class="side-text">
Deposits, moving costs, transportation, employment timing and temporary expenses.
</div>
</div>

<h3 style="margin-top:2rem;">Possible Research Paths</h3>

<ul class="side-list">
<li>Career mobility</li>
<li>Remote work</li>
<li>Apprenticeships</li>
<li>Workforce programs</li>
<li>Professional associations</li>
<li>Small business ecosystems</li>
<li>Educational opportunities</li>
<li>Cost-of-living comparisons</li>
<li>Regional industry clusters</li>
<li>Community resources</li>
</ul>

</aside>

</div>

